---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Section from '@/components/layout/Section.astro';
import Closing from '@/components/reference/Closing.astro';
import Button from '@/components/ui/Button.astro';
import { getPortfolioSlugs, getPortfolioEntry } from '@/lib/portfolio';
export async function getStaticPaths() { return (await getPortfolioSlugs()).map(slug => ({ params: { slug } })); }
const entry = await getPortfolioEntry(Astro.params.slug!);
if (!entry) return Astro.redirect('/portfolio');
const steps = [
  { title: 'Start with the label.', body: 'Every check starts on the product. The Ruzuku label gives the buyer two routes: scan the QR code with the Hakiki app, or send the printed code by SMS.' },
  { title: 'Send a simple message.', body: 'The buyer texts the product keyword and verification code to 15054. For Ruzuku, that means “Ruzuku” followed by the code. This route works without a smartphone or dedicated app.' },
  { title: 'Read the result.', body: 'The reply tells the buyer whether the code is recognised and returns product details, including manufacturing and expiry dates. An invalid code returns a contact for further help, giving the buyer a next step before using the product.' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title="Hakiki: product trust through simple SMS" description="Adopted by the Government of Tanzania, Hakiki makes product traceability and verification accessible by SMS, helping communities avoid counterfeit inputs and supporting businesses and regulators." class="reference-page">
  <Section class="reference-page-intro hakiki-story-intro">
    <a href="/portfolio" class="editorial-link">← Portfolio</a>
    <div class="hakiki-story-header">
      <div><p class="reference-label">Hakiki · A Vuka venture</p><h1 class="hakiki-story-title">Product trust.<br />Within reach.</h1><p class="reference-lead">Hakiki makes product traceability and verification accessible in low-income communities through ordinary SMS. The person checking a product needs no smartphone, app or internet connection.</p></div>
      <div class="hakiki-story-identity"><img src={entry.data.logo} width="300" height="112" alt="Hakiki" /><dl><div><dt>Focus</dt><dd>Product traceability & authentication</dd></div><div><dt>Market</dt><dd>Tanzania</dd></div><div><dt>Built through</dt><dd>Studio Origination</dd></div><div><dt>Access</dt><dd>SMS-first, with QR access</dd></div><div><dt>Adoption</dt><dd>Government of Tanzania</dd></div></dl></div>
    </div>
  </Section>
  <Section variant="sand" class="reference-section">
    <div class="hakiki-story-editorial"><div><p class="reference-label">01 / The problem</p><h2 class="reference-h2">Fake inputs.<br />Real losses.</h2></div><div class="hakiki-story-reading"><p class="reference-lead">For a smallholder farmer, a counterfeit input can put an entire growing season at risk.</p><p>Fake seed, fertiliser and crop-protection products threaten the money and work invested in the next harvest. Evidence from Tanzania and neighbouring Uganda shows why authenticity matters, even though the scale varies by product, place and year.</p><p>A familiar package cannot answer the buyer’s question: is this input genuine? Farmers need a check before use. Businesses need to protect trust in their products. Regulators need traceability that reaches the person buying the input.</p></div></div>
    <div class="hakiki-evidence" aria-label="Country-specific evidence on counterfeit agricultural inputs">
      <article><h3 class="reference-label">Tanzania · Historical estimate, 2013</h3><p class="hakiki-evidence-number">25–30%</p><p>Estimated fake share of certified seed used in Tanzania. A historical USAID estimate from 2013, cited by the World Bank in 2022.</p><a href="https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/099940006282220953/pdf/P1770630615f6e0e0bd4f0286e88583775.pdf#page=57" class="hakiki-source">World Bank: Tanzania Agriculture Public Expenditure Review (2022), paragraph 120 ↗</a></article>
      <article><h3 class="reference-label">Uganda · Government plan, 2025</h3><p class="hakiki-evidence-number">Up to 30%</p><p>Estimated counterfeit share of the agro-inputs market, reported in Uganda’s Fourth National Development Plan, published in March 2025.</p><a href="https://agriculture.go.ug/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Fourth-National-Development-Plan-NDPIV.pdf#page=114" class="hakiki-source">Government of Uganda: Fourth National Development Plan (2025), paragraph 187 ↗</a></article>
    </div>
    <div class="hakiki-evidence-context"><p>These estimates cover different products and periods. They are not a current East Africa-wide prevalence rate.</p><p>Quality also varies by market: a 2021 Tanzanian fertiliser study found good nutrient quality in its sample, despite farmers’ concerns about adulteration. For Hakiki, this reinforces the need to address the trust gap as well as counterfeit risk. <a href="https://www.gov.uk/research-for-development-outputs/misperceived-quality-fertiliser-in-tanzania" class="hakiki-source">Michelson et al. (2021), research summary ↗</a></p></div>
  </Section>
  <Section class="reference-section">
    <div class="hakiki-story-editorial"><div><p class="reference-label">02 / The solution</p><h2 class="reference-h2">A basic phone.<br />A better-informed decision.</h2></div><div class="hakiki-story-reading"><p class="reference-lead">Product trust should not depend on the kind of phone you own or whether you can get online.</p><p>Hakiki connects a code on the product with a verification service reached through ordinary SMS. A smallholder farmer can check agricultural inputs before use, helping them avoid counterfeit products without downloading an app or buying mobile data.</p><p>QR access offers an additional route for app users. Both start with the product label, connecting the buyer’s check with the product information businesses maintain and regulators need. Built in Tanzania, the approach addresses a wider challenge in low-income countries: making traceability useful to the people at the end of the supply chain.</p></div></div>
    <figure class="hakiki-label-example"><div class="hakiki-label-art"><div class="hakiki-product-package"><img src="/assets/portfolio/hakiki/product-label.png" width="542" height="538" alt="Product sticker from the Ruzuku government-subsidised sunflower seed traceability project, with a QR code, scratch panel, Hakiki branding and SMS instructions for 15054." loading="lazy" /></div></div><figcaption><p class="reference-label">On the package</p><h3 class="reference-h3">Verification travels with the product.</h3><p>The product sticker is attached directly to the packaging, giving buyers SMS instructions and a QR code to verify the product. This Ruzuku sticker comes from our project to trace sunflower seeds distributed under Tanzania’s government subsidy programme.</p><p class="hakiki-example-note">Ruzuku sunflower seed project: the actual product sticker shown on illustrative packaging.</p></figcaption></figure>
  </Section>
  <Section variant="sand" class="reference-section">
    <p class="reference-label mb-6">03 / How it works</p><h2 class="reference-h2">A label. A message.<br />An answer.</h2>
    <div class="hakiki-workflow"><ol>{steps.map((step,i) => <li><span class="reference-label" aria-hidden="true">{String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}</span><div><h3 class="reference-h3">{step.title}</h3><p>{step.body}</p></div></li>)}</ol><figure class="hakiki-sms-example"><img src="/assets/portfolio/hakiki/authentication-sms.png" width="430" height="942" alt="Historical SMS test with 15054: an invalid Ruzuku code returns a support message; a recognised code returns Yara Prilated Urea 46-0-0 50 Kg with manufacturing and expiry dates." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Historical SMS test conversation in Swahili. The product codes and 2024–2025 dates are examples.</figcaption></figure></div>
  </Section>
  <Section class="reference-section">
    <div class="hakiki-story-editorial"><div><p class="reference-label">04 / Impact</p><h2 class="reference-h2">Community access.<br />Government adoption.</h2></div><div class="hakiki-story-reading"><p class="reference-lead">Hakiki has now been adopted by the Government of Tanzania.</p><p>Deployment relationships include TOSCI, ASA, TTCL and AATF. Across these relationships, Hakiki connects accessible consumer verification with the needs of agricultural businesses, regulators and telecommunications providers.</p></div></div>
    <div class="hakiki-impact-grid"><div><h3 class="reference-h3">For communities</h3><p>A practical way to check agricultural inputs before use. SMS access gives smallholder farmers and other buyers a way to make more informed decisions, without needing a smartphone or internet connection.</p></div><div><h3 class="reference-h3">For businesses</h3><p>A way for customers to verify products against their records. This helps legitimate producers and suppliers demonstrate authenticity, protect brand trust and support product traceability.</p></div><div><h3 class="reference-h3">For regulators</h3><p>A shared basis for product verification and oversight. Connecting checks with product records supports traceability and gives authorities information to help identify issues and guide follow-up.</p></div></div>
    {entry.data.clientLogos && <div class="hakiki-deployment"><p class="reference-label">Deployment relationships</p><div>{entry.data.clientLogos.map(logo => <img src={logo.src} alt={logo.name} width="150" height="80" loading="lazy" />)}</div></div>}
  </Section>
  <Section variant="sand" class="reference-section"><div class="hakiki-story-editorial"><div><p class="reference-label">05 / The studio’s role</p><h2 class="reference-h2">Built around the people<br />who need it most.</h2></div><div class="hakiki-story-reading"><p class="reference-lead">Hakiki was built through Vuka’s Studio Origination model.</p><p>Vuka brought product development, regulatory navigation and the route to market into the same build. Basic-phone access, local language, variable connectivity and the realities of agricultural supply chains shaped the experience from the start.</p><p>Hakiki’s value lies in connecting those realities: a check communities can access, product trust businesses can support and traceability regulators can work with. Government adoption is a milestone in a product built to make verification part of everyday decisions.</p><Button href="/work-with-us?type=partner">Build for wider access ↗</Button></div></div></Section>
  <Closing />
</BaseLayout>
